Give the boot interrupt stack room for the page fault path

The early boot runs on the interrupt stack: start.s points SP_EL0 at
intstack_top, and the kernel stays there until the first thread stack is
registered.  A data abort taken there is answered by the page fault path,
whose frames are deeper than the 16 KiB the stack has, so the stack runs out
and the fault that ends the boot is the push that crossed the guard page
rather than the fault that asked for the page.

64 KiB leaves room for the boot chain and the fault path together.  The
guard pages around the stack stay the same size, so an overflow is still
caught by the page the push lands in.  Only the static stack grows; the one
cpu_init() allocates per cpu keeps the size it has.

---
--- a/osfmk/arm/data.s
+++ b/osfmk/arm/data.s
@@ -46,7 +46,16 @@
 	/* IRQ stack */
 	.globl  EXT(intstack)						// Boot processor IRQ stack
 LEXT(intstack)
-	.space	(INTSTACK_SIZE_NUM)
+	/*
+	 * nio: the early boot runs on this stack -- start.s points SP_EL0 at
+	 * intstack_top and the kernel stays there until the first thread stack
+	 * is registered.  A data abort taken there is answered by the page fault
+	 * path, whose frames do not fit in 16 KiB: the stack runs out and the
+	 * fault that ends the boot is the push that crossed the guard page, not
+	 * the fault that asked for the page.  Four times the size leaves room for
+	 * the boot chain and the fault path together.
+	 */
+	.space	(INTSTACK_SIZE_NUM * 4)
 	.globl  EXT(intstack_top)
 LEXT(intstack_top)
 
